//? Title: Memetic weighted MAX-CUT search
//? Subtitle: Actual RNG draws, sequential local flips and one retained population trajectory
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Weighted MAX-CUT assigns each graph vertex to one of two sides to maximize
crossing-edge weight. A population heuristic combines random partitions,
selection, crossover, mutation and local refinement, but does not generally
certify a global optimum. We expose actual native candidate/RNG/flip histories,
retain the original sixteen-vertex graph and compare it with complete
small-graph enumeration, while separating request completion from optimality.
)

\toc

> Objective, local gain and population steps

A binary partition s assigns each vertex0 or1. Sum each undirected weighted edge
once when its endpoints differ. Flipping vertex u changes only incident edges:
an edge currently internal becomes crossing and contributes +w; a crossing edge
becomes internal and contributes −w. Thus the real row-order gain test is:

$$
\Delta_u=\sum_{(u,v)}w_{uv}\bigl(1-2\mathbf1[s_u\ne s_v]\bigr).
$$
\alttext(The single-vertex flip gain adds internal edge weights and subtracts crossing edge weights.)

The native local pass checks vertices in increasing order and immediately flips
when the computed delta exceeds1e−12, so later checks see earlier accepted
flips. Repeat until a complete sweep makes no flip. For exact arithmetic, every
accepted flip strictly increases a finite-state objective; floating computations
use the stated tolerance and are reported as native decisions, not exact-real
certificates.

Initialize random binary partitions and locally refine them. Each offspring
selects two parents by separate three-draw strict-fitness tournaments, performs
uniform per-bit crossover, mutates each bit with probability1/N, then locally
refines. Carry the first strict population winner unchanged into the next
population before creating other offspring. This elitism makes the actual
population-best objective nondecreasing; it does not prove the heuristic reaches
the global maximum or outperforms an unmeasured baseline.

> A tiny actual sequential delta

A unit-weight triangle has exact maximum cut two. The native trace retains
actual candidate bits, every RNG draw and each sequential local check. The table
below reads the first actual candidate’s full local-step span; the diagram reads
its first actual before/delta/after decision rather than assuming every check
flips.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/memetic_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::MemeticGraph graph{{{1, 1}, {2, 1}}, {{0, 1}, {2, 1}},
                                       {{0, 1}, {1, 1}}};
    auto t = dm::algorithms::memetic_max_cut_trace(3, graph, 4, 2, 1);
    auto &i = t.individuals.front();
    std::print("| actual global sweep | actual vertex | actual old side | actual delta | actual flip | " "actual new side |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t k = i.first_local; k < i.past_local; ++k) {
        auto &s = t.local_steps[k];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.sweep, s.vertex,
                   s.before, s.delta, s.flipped, s.after);
    }
    auto &s = t.local_steps[i.first_local];
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("before", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual vertex {} old side\n{}", s.vertex, s.before)).node("delta", {6, 0}, std::format("Actual row-order gain\n{}, flip {}", s.delta, s.flipped)).node("after", {12, 0}, std::format("Actual resulting side\n{}", s.after)).edge("before", "delta").edge("delta", "after");
    d.save_svg("build/memetic_tiny.svg");
}
```
