\alttext(The full-enumeration optimum is a comparator; incomplete request status is retained even when a feasible cut happens to attain it.)

> Verification and references

Native tests exhaust761 small integer-weighted graphs through four vertices,
compare complete native enumeration with independent full partition enumeration,
and verify heuristic feasibility and actual terminal computed gains without
demanding universal global success. They replay every RNG state/use, parent
tournament, crossover/mutation bit, sequential local delta/flip, candidate
endpoint and installed population, including elitist retention and capture
counts. The original56-edge fixture, objective195 and prefix-generation results
are retained. Work/capture limits, empty/population-one/seed-zero cases, signed
and parallel edges, symmetry/finite/shape/population guards, ownership and
absolute-range overflow are covered. Existing heuristic and formatting
regressions remain. The shared ten-report batch passes 71 CTest cases, 29
directly instrumented ASan/UBSan cases, 130 compiled and executed public
examples, 598/598 scoped documentation definitions and ten representative
hovers. Independent Python arbitrary-precision, hashlib, sorted-window, RNG,
polynomial, graph and full-assignment references verify 224,229 actual records.
Frozen comparisons preserve ordinary legacy outputs, including selected clique
identities, original density graphs and memetic partitions. Twelve checkout
headers and twelve identically installed headers compile independently; an
installed-header native consumer links and runs. All four complete programs and
all four native figures pass execution and visual review, and HTML/Markdown
exports preserve computed results and identical image bytes. Receipts:
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-224-233/`.

The report uses the standard memetic combination of population exploration and
individual local refinement. It derives the native MAX-CUT gain formula and
states every selection/mutation/termination convention explicitly. The sole
global certificate shown is complete enumeration on the specified small fixture;
no broad optimum or superiority claim is inferred from the heuristic name.

Historical context: Moscato (1989), *On Evolution, Search, Optimization, Genetic
Algorithms and Martial Arts: Towards Memetic Algorithms*, C3P Report826. The
author’s [later primary paper, reference22|https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.00624],
verifies that bibliographic attribution. The native MAX-CUT recipe and exact
operational choices are specified in this report.
